From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 06:05:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] util/queue: destroy the finish barrier after every thread has
 left it

util_queue_finish() puts one util_queue_finish_execute job per thread
behind a util_barrier on its stack, and each job destroyed the barrier
when util_barrier_wait() returned true. With pthread barriers that is
one thread (PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD), but the mutex + condvar
fallback (Windows, macOS) returned true in every thread. Worse, the
thread that releases the barrier gets there first: it broadcasts,
unlocks and destroys the mutex while the threads it has just woken
still have to take the mutex again to leave cnd_wait().

On Windows the mutex is a CRITICAL_SECTION: DeleteCriticalSection()
zeroes it, the woken thread finds it "locked" with no debug info and
faults in RtlpWaitOnCriticalSection (inc [DebugInfo+24h], DebugInfo
NULL). NVK hits it in vkDestroyInstance -> disk_cache_destroy ->
util_queue_finish whenever the shader cache queue has grown to two or
more threads, i.e. on every run that wrote new cache entries: 111 of
150 vk_offscreen_test runs with an empty cache crashed with
0xC0000005 in ntdll.

The jobs no longer destroy the barrier; util_queue_finish() does, after
it has waited for every job's fence (signalled only once the job has
returned from util_barrier_wait()). The fallback util_barrier_wait()
returns true in one thread only, like pthread_barrier_wait().
---
 src/util/u_queue.c  | 13 +++++++++++--
 src/util/u_thread.c |  6 +++++-
 2 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/util/u_queue.c b/src/util/u_queue.c
index 6c13521..5d11508 100644
--- a/src/util/u_queue.c
+++ b/src/util/u_queue.c
@@ -522,8 +522,13 @@ static void
 util_queue_finish_execute(void *data, void *gdata, int num_thread)
 {
    util_barrier *barrier = data;
-   if (util_barrier_wait(barrier))
-      util_barrier_destroy(barrier);
+
+   /* util_queue_finish destroys the barrier once every thread has left it.
+    * Destroying it here, in whichever thread util_barrier_wait picks, can
+    * pull the mutex out from under the threads it has just woken up: they
+    * still have to take the mutex again to leave util_barrier_wait.
+    */
+   util_barrier_wait(barrier);
 }
 
 void
@@ -729,6 +734,10 @@ util_queue_finish(struct util_queue *queue)
       util_queue_fence_destroy(&fences[i]);
    }
 
+   /* Each fence is signalled after its job has returned from
+    * util_barrier_wait, so no thread uses the barrier any more.
+    */
+   util_barrier_destroy(&barrier);
    free(fences);
 }
 
diff --git a/src/util/u_thread.c b/src/util/u_thread.c
index d1cf085..e0401ab 100644
--- a/src/util/u_thread.c
+++ b/src/util/u_thread.c
@@ -233,6 +233,8 @@ void util_barrier_destroy(util_barrier *barrier)
 
 bool util_barrier_wait(util_barrier *barrier)
 {
+   bool serial = false;
+
    mtx_lock(&barrier->mutex);
 
    assert(barrier->waiters < barrier->count);
@@ -248,11 +250,13 @@ bool util_barrier_wait(util_barrier *barrier)
       barrier->waiters = 0;
       barrier->sequence++;
       cnd_broadcast(&barrier->condvar);
+      serial = true;
    }
 
    mtx_unlock(&barrier->mutex);
 
-   return true;
+   /* Like PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD: true in exactly one thread. */
+   return serial;
 }
 
 #endif
